<BaseLayout title="About XOCE" description="Xef’s Open Computing Ecosystem: practical software, thoughtful interfaces and room to explore new technology.">
  <main id="main" class="wrap section page-main"><header class="page-heading about-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Xef’s Open Computing Ecosystem</p><h1>Technology should<br />make room for you.</h1><p class="lead">XOCE brings practical apps and computing experiments under one roof. An independent project by Xef, built around a simple question: could this be easier to use?</p></header>
    <section class="about-intro"><div class="about-monogram" aria-hidden="true"><span>✳</span><strong>XOCE</strong><small>OPEN TO POSSIBILITY</small></div><div class="prose"><h2>Ambitious ideas. Everyday usefulness.</h2><p>The project’s direction spans Linux and KDE Plasma, Android, and PebbleOS. The first documented software collection is for Pebble: Dynamic, Dashboard, Timer and Open Activity, with shared developer libraries behind them.</p><p>Alongside that practical work, XOCE is a place to explore new interaction models, modern standards and better ways to move between tools. The goal is software that feels quick, clear and comfortable to live with.</p><p>Each project has its own purpose, code repository and release cycle. Shared principles give the collection a recognisable character while leaving space for the conventions of each platform.</p></div></section>
    <section class="section about-principles"><p class="eyebrow">What we care about</p><h2>Keep the useful parts.</h2><div class="guide-grid"><article class="principle-card"><span class="principle-number">01</span><h3>People before mechanisms.</h3><p>Make common tasks understandable. Ask for information when it matters. Give people clear feedback and control.</p></article><article class="principle-card"><span class="principle-number">02</span><h3>Performance is part of design.</h3><p>Fast responses, readable screens and careful battery use belong in the design from the beginning.</p></article><article class="principle-card"><span class="principle-number">03</span><h3>Experiment with a purpose.</h3><p>Try new approaches, explain the tradeoffs, and keep what makes the experience better.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="contribute-panel" id="contribute"><div><p class="eyebrow">Get involved</p><h2>Bring a useful idea.</h2><p>Try a development build, report a clear bug, improve a guide, or build an activity plugin. The individual project repositories are the place to share issues and contributions.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button button-primary" href="/projects/">Find a project <Icon name="arrow" size={18}/></a><a class="button button-plain" href="/docs/develop/activities/">Build an activity <Icon name="arrow" size={18}/></a></div></div><div class="contribute-details"><Icon name="code" size={34}/><h3>Be clear about what’s ready.</h3><p>Project pages distinguish development builds, embedded libraries and experiments. Compatibility, limitations and licences are documented per project.</p><a class="text-link" href="/docs/project/licensing/">Licensing & credits <Icon name="arrow" size={17}/></a></div></section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
